WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.366 --> 00:00:08.860
On October 4th, 2020, epidemiologists from
Harvard, Oxford, and Stanford, authored

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:10.728
The Great Barrington Declaration. 

00:00:10.728 --> 00:00:14.725
Because of “grave concerns about the damaging physical and mental health impacts

00:00:14.725 --> 00:00:20.939
of the prevailing COVID-19 policies,” they argued that all COVID-19 lockdowns should end.

00:00:20.939 --> 00:00:25.080
Instead, we should adopt a strategy they call “Focused Protection,”

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:28.365
which is aimed at protecting elderly and vulnerable populations

00:00:28.365 --> 00:00:31.330
while allowing everyone else to resume normal life.

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:35.825
The proponents of Focused Protection say it’s the most compassionate approach 

00:00:35.825 --> 00:00:39.930
“to minimize mortality and social harm until we reach herd immunity,” 

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:43.724
which is the goal of all COVID-19 mitigation strategies.

00:00:43.724 --> 00:00:47.845
This is the saner approach, the more moral approach, the more scientifically based approach.

00:00:47.845 --> 00:00:51.404
Critics of the Declaration issued a counter memorandum stating that,

00:00:51.404 --> 00:00:58.179
“Any pandemic management strategy relying upon immunity from natural infections for COVID-19 is flawed...

00:00:58.179 --> 00:01:05.802
Uncontrolled transmission in younger people risks significant morbidity and mortality across the whole population.”

00:01:05.802 --> 00:01:08.889
Should the Coronavirus lockdowns be lifted and replaced

00:01:08.889 --> 00:01:13.590
with a targeted strategy that protects the old and other high-risk groups?

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:19.013
That was the subject of an online Soho Forum Debate on Sunday December 13th.

00:01:19.013 --> 00:01:23.746
It featured Martin Kulldorff, a Harvard biostatistician and epidemiologist

00:01:23.746 --> 00:01:27.051
and co-author of the Great Barrington Declaration

00:01:27.051 --> 00:01:31.844
versus&nbsp;Andrew Noymer, an associate professor of Population Health and Disease Prevention

00:01:31.844 --> 00:01:38.362
at University of California, Irvine, who signed the so-called John Snow Memorandum written in response.

00:01:38.362 --> 00:01:41.797
Here’s Martin Kulldorf and Andrew Noymer in an online debate

00:01:41.797 --> 00:01:45.360
moderated by Soho Forum Director Gene Epstein.

